      "role": "user",
      "content": "Definition: Understandability refers to how easily developers can comprehend and maintain the test code.\n\nTask: Improve the understandability of the test `JavaVersionTest_testGetJavaVersion.java` (path:- /Users/tenghaha/Projects/New_Agent_understandability_improvement/data/original/commons-lang/manual/testcases/JavaVersionTest/JavaVersionTest_testGetJavaVersion.java). The refactored code must be syntactically correct and compilable against the provided CUT signatures. Do not alter the runtime behaviour of the original test: the same methods must be called with the same arguments and produce the same expected outcomes.\n"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll read the original test and the CUT source to understand what needs to be improved.Now I'll write the improved test with better grouping and explanatory comments.Now I'll run the compile check.Let me check the compile script to understand where it expects the file.The compile script expects the file in the working directory. I need to write the file there instead.The improved test compiles successfully. The changes made for better understandability:\n\n1. **Grouped assertions logically** into three named categories with explanatory comments: Android legacy (`0.9`), legacy 1.x format (Java 1.1–1.8), and modern major-version format (Java 9–27).\n2. **Explained the edge cases** with inline comments: why `\"1.10\"` maps to `JAVA_RECENT` (minor part > .9), why `\"99\"` maps to `JAVA_RECENT` (unknown future version per LANG-1384), and what the wrapper method test verifies.\n3. **Removed the unused imports** (`assertFalse`, `assertTrue`) that were present in the original but never referenced."
    }
  ],
  "last_code": "package org.apache.commons.lang3;\n\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\n\npublic class JavaVersionTest_testGetJavaVersion extends AbstractLangTest {\n\n    @Test\n    void testGetJavaVersion() throws Exception {\n        // Legacy format: Android's non-standard \"0.9\" version string maps to JAVA_0_9\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_0_9, JavaVersion.get(\"0.9\"), \"0.9 failed\");\n\n        // Legacy format: Java 1.1–1.8 used \"1.x\" version strings\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_1, JavaVersion.get(\"1.1\"), \"1.1 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_2, JavaVersion.get(\"1.2\"), \"1.2 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_3, JavaVersion.get(\"1.3\"), \"1.3 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_4, JavaVersion.get(\"1.4\"), \"1.4 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_5, JavaVersion.get(\"1.5\"), \"1.5 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_6, JavaVersion.get(\"1.6\"), \"1.6 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_7, JavaVersion.get(\"1.7\"), \"1.7 failed\");\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_1_8, JavaVersion.get(\"1.8\"), \"1.8 failed\");\n\n        // Modern format: Java 9+ uses a plain major version number string\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_9,  JavaVersion.get(\"9\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_10, JavaVersion.get(\"10\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_11, JavaVersion.get(\"11\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_12, JavaVersion.get(\"12\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_13, JavaVersion.get(\"13\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_14, JavaVersion.get(\"14\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_15, JavaVersion.get(\"15\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_16, JavaVersion.get(\"16\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_17, JavaVersion.get(\"17\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_18, JavaVersion.get(\"18\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_19, JavaVersion.get(\"19\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_20, JavaVersion.get(\"20\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_21, JavaVersion.get(\"21\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_22, JavaVersion.get(\"22\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_23, JavaVersion.get(\"23\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_24, JavaVersion.get(\"24\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_25, JavaVersion.get(\"25\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_26, JavaVersion.get(\"26\"));\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_27, JavaVersion.get(\"27\"));\n\n        // \"1.10\" has a minor part > .9 in the legacy range, so it maps to JAVA_RECENT\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_RECENT, JavaVersion.get(\"1.10\"), \"1.10 failed\");\n        // assertNull(\"2.10 unexpectedly worked\", JavaVersion.get(\"2.10\"));\n\n        // getJavaVersion is a wrapper that delegates to get — results must be identical\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.get(\"1.5\"), JavaVersion.getJavaVersion(\"1.5\"), \"Wrapper method failed\");\n\n        // LANG-1384: an unrecognised future version (e.g. \"99\") falls back to JAVA_RECENT\n        assertEquals(JavaVersion.JAVA_RECENT, JavaVersion.get(\"99\"), \"Unhandled\");\n    }\n}\n",
